# Force LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# Without it, a clone on a machine with `core.autocrlf=true` - the Windows Git
# default - gets CRLF working-tree copies of everything git rewrites. That is
# not cosmetic here:
#
#   * shellcheck rejects a CRLF script outright (SC1017), so the local
#     "Shellcheck scripts" task fails on a clean checkout while CI passes,
#     because a Linux runner checks out LF regardless. A check that disagrees
#     with CI depending on who ran it is worse than no check.
#   * `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` is not a valid shebang on Linux. These scripts are
#     run explicitly as `bash script.sh` today, which hides it - but anything
#     that ever executes one directly gets "bad interpreter: no such file".
#   * A CRLF `action.yml` is read by GitHub without complaint, and then every
#     `run:` block it contains carries trailing carriage returns into the shell.
#
# This was not theoretical: the working tree on Windows held CRLF copies and
# the shellcheck layer started failing locally while CI stayed green.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: decide what is text by inspection, normalize it to
# LF in the repository, and check it out as LF everywhere. Nothing here is
# Windows-native and nothing needs CRLF.
#
# This repository never committed CRLF - the blobs have been LF throughout, and
# only the checked-out copies were converted, so `git add --renormalize .` here
# is a no-op. Run it after adding this file to a repo that did commit CRLF; to
# fix a working tree that already has it, re-checkout the affected files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git does not attempt any conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
